Hypothesis
Impacket wmiexec.py is being used for remote execution / lateral movement within EPA Windows environments. Every wmiexec command runs through a cmd.exe /Q /c capture shell that redirects stdout and stderr to \\<host>\ADMIN$\__<epoch> and reads it back over SMB. That redirect string is a tool-inherent, high-fidelity signature: a semi-interactive shell built on the fire-and-forget Win32_Process.Create method must write output somewhere it can read back, and that write appears in the command line of every command. It survives the operator rotating credentials, changing the source host, moving off the 127.0.0.1 default, and switching between password and pass-the-hash auth (only the logon changes, not the execution).
Threat-model relevance: APT27 (Bronze Union) is documented moving laterally with native WMI, and the broader China-nexus living-off-the-land cluster tracked in the EPA threat actor matrix (Volt Typhoon, Flax Typhoon, Salt Typhoon, UNC3886) favors exactly this built-in-tooling tradecraft. wmiexec is also a first-reach tool for any actor operating with valid credentials, so it is worth hunting independent of a specific attribution.


Scope
Windows hosts across EPA components represented in Splunk: OIG, OMS/OITO, Region 1, Region 7 (indexes epa_oig_wineventlog_sec, epa_oms_oito_wineventlog_sec, epa_r1_wineventlog_sec, epa_r7_wineventlog_sec).
All Microsoft Defender for Endpoint (MDE) onboarded Windows endpoints.
Behavior in scope: WMI-based remote command execution and the immediate hands-on-keyboard session that follows it (discovery, defense evasion, credential access).
Time window: Defender Advanced Hunting — last 30 days (retention limit). Splunk — extend per index retention for historical look-back.


Data Sources
Microsoft Defender for Endpoint — Advanced Hunting (DeviceProcessEvents, DeviceFileEvents). Guarantees ProcessCommandLine and InitiatingProcessFileName (parent) on every row with no configuration. ~30-day retention. Coverage limited to onboarded hosts only.
Splunk — Windows Security Event Logs (EventCode 4688). sourcetype WinEventLog. No Sysmon present, so no EventCode 1 — this is EventCode 4688 only. Command-line visibility is contingent on the "Include command line in process creation events" GPO; must be verified before the primary Splunk query can work (see Intelligence Gaps).


